WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.920
Thanks for joining me on fascinating frontiers, Episode 80, coming to you on May 25th, 2026.

00:00:06.740 --> 00:00:11.980
New week, new discoveries, let's see what's happening. NASA's new directive targets a nuclear

00:00:11.980 --> 00:00:18.540
reactor on the Moon by 2030 to support sustained surface operations. The agency will develop a

00:00:18.540 --> 00:00:24.860
fish-in-surface power system for lunar operations under a fresh policy reviving earlier nuclear ambitions.

00:00:25.580 --> 00:00:31.680
Work includes ground testing of nuclear thermal propulsion concepts intended for crude Mars missions

00:00:31.680 --> 00:00:37.600
in the 2040s. The effort draws on studies conducted quietly since the Apollo era,

00:00:37.940 --> 00:00:44.300
but had not received formal direction until now. A reactor would supply continuous power during

00:00:44.300 --> 00:00:50.020
the lunar night when solar arrays produce nothing. Next steps involve selecting industry partners

00:00:50.020 --> 00:00:55.260
for the initial demonstration unit. From steady power on the Moon to the practical problem

00:00:55.260 --> 00:01:00.700
of keeping astronauts clothing clean without water, we turn to another technology that could support

00:01:00.700 --> 00:01:07.460
long surface stays. Researchers are examining a method that uses ionized gas to remove dust and

00:01:07.460 --> 00:01:13.320
microbes from fabrics without water. The technique would address the severe water shortage expected

00:01:13.320 --> 00:01:19.360
during long surface stays. Early lab tests show plasma can break down organic contaminants at

00:01:19.360 --> 00:01:25.020
low temperatures suitable for spacecraft. The approach could extend the usable life of limited

00:01:25.020 --> 00:01:31.180
clothing supplies carried from Earth. Follow-on studies will test material durability after repeated

00:01:31.180 --> 00:01:37.180
exposures. While engineers tackle life support challenges on other worlds, sky watchers on Earth

00:01:37.180 --> 00:01:43.240
have a simple treat coming up later this month. Mercury, Venus, and Mars appear low in the west shortly

00:01:43.240 --> 00:01:48.700
after sunset for observers in the northern hemisphere. Mercury has climbed above the horizon,

00:01:48.700 --> 00:01:54.660
following its recent superior conjunction. Venus remains the brightest of the trio while Mars

00:01:54.660 --> 00:02:00.180
shows a distinct reddish hue. The alignment offers a brief window before the planet's separate

00:02:00.180 --> 00:02:06.560
over subsequent weeks. Observers need only clear western skies and no optical aid for the brighter

00:02:06.560 --> 00:02:13.340
pair. For those who want more than naked eye views, a new remote observatory in west Texas is opening

00:02:13.340 --> 00:02:19.180
access to professional grade instruments. A new ranch-based observatory provides remote access

00:02:19.180 --> 00:02:25.760
to instruments for amateur and student observers. The site offers darker skies than many urban locations

00:02:25.760 --> 00:02:32.220
and automated scheduling software. Users can request imaging time on multiple telescopes without

00:02:32.220 --> 00:02:38.080
traveling to the facility. The project aims to expand access to professional grade equipment for

00:02:38.080 --> 00:02:43.340
educational programs. Early bookings have already filled several nights per week.

00:02:44.340 --> 00:02:49.300
Staying with Canadian Connections, one of our own astronauts is back home briefly before heading

00:02:49.300 --> 00:02:55.500
to space. Kutrick arrived home ahead of final training for the upcoming Crew 13 mission to the

00:02:55.500 --> 00:03:01.000
International Space Station. He will serve as a mission specialist on the flight scheduled for later

00:03:01.000 --> 00:03:07.200
this year. The visit allows time with family before departure to the launch site. Canadian Space

00:03:07.200 --> 00:03:13.080
Agency officials confirmed he remains on track for the assigned seat. Kutrick previously supported

00:03:13.080 --> 00:03:18.960
ground operations for earlier commercial crew flights. Another Canadian space effort is also moving

00:03:18.960 --> 00:03:24.640
forward through an international partnership. Reaction dynamics reach an agreement to explore

00:03:24.640 --> 00:03:30.480
sea-based launch infrastructure for its aurora rocket. The vehicle is designed for small satellite

00:03:30.480 --> 00:03:36.740
missions from high latitude sites. Hanwa Ocean will contribute engineering expertise on floating

00:03:36.740 --> 00:03:42.360
platforms. The collaboration targets eventual operations from Canadian coastal waters.

00:03:43.120 --> 00:03:49.160
Regulatory and environmental reviews remain the next milestones. From hardware on the ground to

00:03:49.160 --> 00:03:54.620
software that could help us recognize life elsewhere, we look at an AI study for the Dragonfly

00:03:54.620 --> 00:04:01.180
mission. Simulation show current instruments on Dragonfly could overlook weak chemical signals produced

00:04:01.180 --> 00:04:07.360
by simple life. Machine learning models trained on known false positives improved discrimination

00:04:07.360 --> 00:04:13.580
between biological and abiotic patterns. The study focused on Titan's surface materials that the

00:04:13.580 --> 00:04:19.360
rotor craft will sample after arrival. Researchers note that similar algorithms could be uploaded to

00:04:19.360 --> 00:04:25.500
existing spacecraft already in route. Additional validation against laboratory analogues is planned

00:04:25.500 --> 00:04:32.040
before flight software updates. NASA's directive to place a nuclear reactor on the moon by 2030

00:04:32.040 --> 00:04:39.040
revives technology studied since the 1960s but never flown on a crude mission. A fishing system

00:04:39.040 --> 00:04:44.220
would deliver steady kilowatts through the two-week lunar night when solar power drops to zero.

00:04:44.940 --> 00:04:50.440
Engineers must still solve shielding dust tolerance and remote startup challenges before the first

00:04:50.440 --> 00:04:56.080
unit lands. The same power source could later support propellant production for return trips.

00:04:56.780 --> 00:05:02.520
One open question is how quickly the reactor can be qualified for human proximity after years of

00:05:02.520 --> 00:05:10.700
dormancy. You know what is fascinating about this? A single kilogram of uranium 235 contains roughly

00:05:10.700 --> 00:05:16.700
3 million times the energy of a kilogram of gasoline, enough to run a modest lunar base for months

00:05:16.700 --> 00:05:23.160
without refueling. Picture a compact reactor lowered onto the regolith. Control rod slide out,

00:05:23.440 --> 00:05:28.520
fishing begins, and heat travels through heat pipes to sterling engines that spin at a few

00:05:28.520 --> 00:05:34.560
hundred revolutions per minute to generate electricity. The entire assembly might weigh only a few tons

00:05:34.560 --> 00:05:39.900
yet produce tens of kilowatts continuously, independent of sunlight or dust storms.

00:05:40.700 --> 00:05:46.420
On Earth, we take grid-scale cooling for granted. On the moon, the same reactor must reject waste

00:05:46.420 --> 00:05:51.300
heat through radiators that glow at several hundred degrees against the three Kelvin sky.

00:05:52.240 --> 00:05:57.620
The remaining puzzle is how to keep neutron economy stable when the reactor sits on a surface that

00:05:57.620 --> 00:06:03.480
reflects some neutrons back into the core, an effect that changes with exact landing orientation

00:06:03.480 --> 00:06:09.360
and local terrain. Before we go, keep an eye on further updates about the Dragonfly mission

00:06:09.360 --> 00:06:15.000
software tests. That covers today's space and science news. Share this with a fellow space

00:06:15.000 --> 00:06:20.740
enthusiast if you found it interesting. I'm Patrick and Vancouver, see you tomorrow. This episode

00:06:20.740 --> 00:06:25.700
used AI voice synthesis of my voice. Editorial selection and analysis are my own.
